[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: FRRouting on router2 (bgpd/zebra/vtysh). The routing control plane on router2 is down.

Mechanism: With bgpd/zebra stopped on router2, the eBGP peering across 193.10.11.0/24 is not established. Router1 therefore has no route to 200.1.1.0/24, so replies from pc1’s side cannot return to pc2. pc2 can still reach its local gateway (router2: 200.1.1.1), but end-to-end traffic fails because the path is asymmetric/absent. Router2’s Linux kernel still shows a proto “bgp” route to 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1, likely left behind when FRR died, which does not restore bidirectional connectivity.

Localization: Router2’s control-plane processes. Evidence:
- On router2, vtysh fails completely (“vtysh_failed”), and FRR status shows “inactive (dead)” with both zebra and bgpd not running (frr_show_running_config: “zebra is not running”, “bgpd is not running”).
- On router1, FRR is up and BGP neighbor 193.10.11.2 (AS 2) is stuck in Active (not Established), and its routing table has only connected routes (no 200.1.1.0/24), matching lost reachability to pc2.
- pc2->pc1 ping times out, while links/interfaces are up on both routers.

Root cause: The FRR service on router2 stopped/crashed, bringing down the BGP adjacency.

Corrective fix: Restart FRR on router2 (ensure watchfrr, zebra, and bgpd are running), then verify “show ip bgp summary” is Established on both routers and that routes to 195.11.14.0/24 and 200.1.1.0/24 are present. Enable/verify autostart and inspect /var/log/frr/frr.log for the reason of the crash to prevent recurrence.
=== END ===